## What I Learned

This week I studied the basics of Linux: what it is, how it differs from other operating systems, and how to work with it from the command line.

Linux is everywhere. It runs web servers, phones, routers, smart TVs, supercomputers and most of the cloud, which is why Linux skills are needed in almost every area of IT, from DevOps and cybersecurity to networking and development.

Strictly speaking, "Linux" is only the kernel. The kernel is the core of the operating system: it sits between the hardware and the software and manages the CPU, memory, storage and devices. Everything else (the shell, tools, desktop and package manager) is added on top by a distribution.

Linux is also ooen source. Its source code is public, so anyone can read it, modify it and share it under licences like the GPL. This is why Linux improves quickly, costs nothing to use, and is trusted for security-sensitive work: many people can inspect the code.

A distribution (distro) bundles the Linux kernel with software, tools, a package manager and often a desktop environment. Examples include Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora and Red Hat. Distros fall into families, which matters mostly for package management. The Debian family (Debian, Ubuntu) uses `.deb` packages with `apt` and `dpkg`, while the Red Hat family (RHEL, Fedora, CentOS) uses `.rpm` packages with `dnf`, `yum` and `rpm`. 

An operating system is the software that manages a computer's hardware and software resources. It schedules programs so many tasks appear to run at the same time (multitasking) and provides standard services programs can request, such as printing or file access. Comparing the main options, Windows is the most common on desktops with strong software and gaming support, macOS is a polished Unix-based system tied to Apple hardware, and Linux is free, flexible and dominant on servers and in the cloud. Linux is also used in embedded systems, meaning single-purpose devices like routers and set-top boxes.

Linux can be used entirely from the command line, which is fast, scriptable and works on remote servers that have no desktop. A **shell** is the program that reads the commands I type and passes them to the operating system, and Bash is the most common one. 

Package managers install, update and remove software and handle dependencies automatically. 

Finally, most cloud servers run Linux. Cloud providers let you rent virtual machines running distributions like Ubuntu, which you then manage over the command line. This connects directly to the DevOps and cloud work I'm building towards.



